    pr_ref:  # e9fc
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-27 while landing WI-lutuh's 20 rust grants, on the live CLI.\n\nINV-tabaf established the principle for OVERLAYS and is satisfied: when a\nproject-local overlay declares module_completeness, the run names the vouched\nmodules, because \"an overlay's completeness grant is the most powerful line a\nuser can write and the least visible\".\n\nTHE SHIPPED-CATALOGUE HALF WAS NEVER ASKED. A grant in a shipped catalogue is\nthe same closed-world claim, turns the same gate off, and is disclosed by\nNOTHING.\n\n  $ hypergumbo verify-claims <rust repo> --claims claims.yaml --json\n  # rust.yaml now carries 20 module_completeness entries\n  $ grep -il 'completeness grant\\|vouched' run.err\n  # (no match, on any of the five rust repos in the A/B cohort)\n\nThe stderr carries the in_progress catalogue warnings and nothing about grants.\nReproduced on all five repos of the WI-lutuh A/B (arti, encrypted-dns-server,\ngit, rage, ripgrep), NEW arm.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW THAN IT DID YESTERDAY. Until today exactly one shipped\ncatalogue carried grants (python, 76, since May) and rust carried none. Rust\nnow carries 20, and the arc puts cpp next, so the shipped population is\ngrowing. A reader who is told \"no fs_write chains found\" has no way to learn\nthat the answer rests on 20 modules somebody vouched for in a file they have\nnot read.\n\nNOT A DOC BUG. docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md s\"A module_completeness grant in your\noverlay turns a gate OFF\" is careful to say OVERLAY, so it is accurate as\nwritten. What is missing is any statement, anywhere the user reaches, that the\nSHIPPED catalogue makes the same kind of claim.\n\nTWO SHAPES, AND THE CHOICE IS NOT OBVIOUS -- which is why this is filed rather\nthan fixed:\n  (a) Disclose shipped grants the way overlay grants are disclosed. Honest, but\n      python alone would print 76 module names on every python run, which is\n      the kind of noise that gets a disclosure ignored or suppressed.\n  (b) Disclose only the grants that were LOAD-BEARING for this verdict -- the\n      granted modules this repo actually called into. Smaller, more useful, and\n      strictly more work: it needs the per-verdict module set the gate already\n      computes, which is where the count in the reason string comes from.\n\n(b) looks right and is the one I would build, but it is a behaviour change to a\nsecurity disclosure and it is not WI-lutuh's scope."  # e9fc
    fields: {}  # e9fc
